WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.934 --> 00:00:02.568
WIL WILLIS: Allen.

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:04.237
Clayton.

00:00:04.304 --> 00:00:06.305
Congratulations, you both made
it through to the final round.

00:00:06.406 --> 00:00:07.941
When you showed
up at this forge,

00:00:08.041 --> 00:00:10.610
you used our tools and
equipment to forge signature

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.378
blades in your own style.

00:00:12.478 --> 00:00:14.447
Now we're sending you
back to your home forge,

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:16.583
where you will have
five days to forge

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:18.418
an iconic weapon from history.

00:00:22.389 --> 00:00:24.523
The boar spear.

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:27.259
WIL WILLIS (VOICEOVER):
Developed as early as 700 AD

00:00:27.326 --> 00:00:29.094
in Europe, the boar
spear was forged

00:00:29.161 --> 00:00:31.297
for both combat and hunting.

00:00:31.363 --> 00:00:33.232
Known at the time
as a "bar spare,"

00:00:33.332 --> 00:00:35.234
the weapon was about
five to six feet long,

00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:38.104
and featured a pair of lugs
below the leaf-shaped blade.

00:00:38.171 --> 00:00:40.506
When hunting, the lugs
would stop a speared boar

00:00:40.606 --> 00:00:42.475
from running up the
length of the weapon,

00:00:42.542 --> 00:00:44.510
and attacking the user.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.946
In combat, the lugs
could be used to hook

00:00:47.046 --> 00:00:49.282
and pull away enemy shields.

00:00:49.348 --> 00:00:51.150
The blade of the spear
could be decorated,

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:54.119
with some featuring finely
engraved scenes of hunts.

00:00:54.219 --> 00:00:56.355
On the first season
of "Game of Thrones,"

00:00:56.455 --> 00:00:58.223
King Robert Baratheon
carried a boar

00:00:58.323 --> 00:01:00.225
spear on his final boar hunt.

00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:01.961
WIL WILLIS: Good
luck, bladesmiths.

00:01:02.061 --> 00:01:03.829
We'll see you in five days.

00:01:03.929 --> 00:01:04.596
 Good luck.

00:01:04.663 --> 00:01:06.966 line:7%
 Good luck.

00:01:07.032 --> 00:01:08.434 line:7%
CLAYTON: Day two.

00:01:08.501 --> 00:01:10.070 line:0%
I really need to figure
out the design of the lugs.

00:01:10.136 --> 00:01:11.437
Boars are pretty strong
animals, so if they don't have

00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:13.306
a wide enough
cross section, they

00:01:13.373 --> 00:01:15.842
could just end up running that
through their body themselves.

00:01:15.942 --> 00:01:19.011
One of the ways I could do it
is cutting a section of the pipe

00:01:19.111 --> 00:01:20.979
out, and bending it out.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:22.281
It's going to take
a lot of work,

00:01:22.348 --> 00:01:23.382
but it's something
that needs to be done.

00:01:23.483 --> 00:01:24.717
I'm battling the
clock on this one.

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:29.622
I've decided to put my lugs
perpendicular to my blade.

00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:32.258
It just made more sense to
have them perpendicular,

00:01:32.358 --> 00:01:34.026
to stop the spear
from over-penetrating.

00:01:34.093 --> 00:01:34.727
It should work.

00:01:37.863 --> 00:01:39.165 line:7%
ALLEN: It's day three.

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:40.967
Time's really crunched.

00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:42.334
I'm just really
behind the eight ball.

00:01:42.401 --> 00:01:44.437
Getting the lugs
on the spear shaft.

00:01:47.006 --> 00:01:50.075
Oh man.

00:01:50.142 --> 00:01:52.077
I believe my blower
has just died.

00:01:52.144 --> 00:01:54.647
My forge burnt out,
the engine died.

00:01:54.714 --> 00:01:57.149
Now I'm going to have
to find a replacement.

00:01:57.216 --> 00:01:58.718
There's still a lot to do.

00:01:58.784 --> 00:02:02.254
I am literally running to the
store to buy another blower.

00:02:02.321 --> 00:02:05.324
Things just really are
not going my way today.

00:02:05.424 --> 00:02:08.160
My forge burnt out,
the engine died.

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.162
Had to run out and lose time.

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:11.730
I had to get a new motor.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:16.536
With all these setbacks, I'm
starting to be a bit worried.

00:02:16.603 --> 00:02:17.971
All right, it works.

00:02:18.037 --> 00:02:19.939
With the way things
have been going today,

00:02:20.039 --> 00:02:21.774
we're coming down to
the wire on time here.

00:02:21.874 --> 00:02:23.042
It could come back to bite me.

00:02:23.142 --> 00:02:24.176
DOUG MARCAIDA: Bladesmiths.

00:02:24.276 --> 00:02:25.879
This is the sharpness test.

00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:27.880
To see how sharp
your boar spears are,

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:29.649
we will test the
tip and the edge

00:02:29.749 --> 00:02:32.418
by stabbing and slashing
through these sandbags.

00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:33.419
 All right.

00:02:33.485 --> 00:02:34.320
DOUG MARCAIDA: Clayton,
you're up first.

00:02:34.387 --> 00:02:34.821
You ready?
- I'm ready.

00:02:48.066 --> 00:02:49.469
DOUG MARCAIDA: Well, Clayton.

00:02:49.536 --> 00:02:53.405
The balance of your tip
lends itself to stabbing.

00:02:53.472 --> 00:02:57.110
Your edge lacerated cleanly
on the moving sandbag.

00:02:57.177 --> 00:02:59.678
What surprised me was the
positioning of your lug.

00:02:59.745 --> 00:03:02.315
Traditionally, they have
it crosswise this way.

00:03:02.415 --> 00:03:03.482
But you have it this way.

00:03:03.549 --> 00:03:05.351
Because of that, it
was hard to index

00:03:05.451 --> 00:03:07.386
when you're cutting through.

00:03:07.453 --> 00:03:09.856
But then again, it will cut.

00:03:09.956 --> 00:03:10.723
 Thank you

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:11.457
 Allen, you're next.

00:03:11.557 --> 00:03:12.024
Ready?

00:03:12.124 --> 00:03:12.792
 Oh yeah.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:29.441
DOUG MARCAIDA: Well, Allen.

00:03:29.508 --> 00:03:31.343
Your spear feels
good to hold on to.

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:33.579
It was easy to index in
the way the edge was.

00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:36.582
So that was a nice slash
and I can penetrate through.

00:03:36.649 --> 00:03:39.151
And in the thrust, it
felt good in the hand.

00:03:39.218 --> 00:03:40.386
 All right.

00:03:40.486 --> 00:03:42.521
 I can see a little
bow in your handle.

00:03:42.622 --> 00:03:45.891
But when we're talking
about the sharpness test,

00:03:45.958 --> 00:03:47.626
this, sir, will cut.

00:03:47.693 --> 00:03:49.696
 All right.

00:03:49.796 --> 00:03:51.898
 Next stop is a kill test.

00:03:51.964 --> 00:03:54.934
To see what kind of lethal
damage your weapon can do,

00:03:55.001 --> 00:03:58.137
I will deliver lethal blows
to this wild boar carcass.

00:03:58.204 --> 00:03:59.071
Clayton, you're up first.

00:03:59.138 --> 00:03:59.872
Are you ready?

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:00.773
 Absolutely.

00:04:14.553 --> 00:04:15.955
DOUG MARCAIDA: Well, Clayton.

00:04:16.022 --> 00:04:19.959
Your boar spear right here
did very well on the thrust,

00:04:20.026 --> 00:04:23.328
penetrating all the way
through, on the low line,

00:04:23.395 --> 00:04:26.065
and on the high line.

00:04:26.132 --> 00:04:29.434
On the edge, you
lacerated cleanly

00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:31.504
and testing the
other side, it cut

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:32.805
all the way through the spine.

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:37.243
This sir, will kill.

00:04:37.343 --> 00:04:38.043
Good job.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:39.211
 Thank you.

00:04:39.278 --> 00:04:40.045
 Allen, you're next.

00:04:40.112 --> 00:04:40.813
You ready?

00:04:40.913 --> 00:04:41.848
 Oh yeah.

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:42.548
DOUG MARCAIDA: Let's do this.

00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:02.735
Well, Allen, let's
talk about your blade.

00:05:02.802 --> 00:05:05.738
On the thrust, it penetrated
all the way through.

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:10.376
On the laceration, on one
side of your edge, boy, wow.

00:05:10.443 --> 00:05:12.778
It almost sliced
all the way through.

00:05:12.878 --> 00:05:17.983
On your other edge, it
lacerated through the spine.

00:05:18.083 --> 00:05:20.118
This piercer will kill.

00:05:20.185 --> 00:05:21.253
 All right.

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:22.655
DAVID BAKER:
Gentlemen, it's been

00:05:22.722 --> 00:05:24.791
said that the sword is
the prince of all weapons,

00:05:24.891 --> 00:05:25.758
but the spear is the king.

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:28.094
To test the strength
of your boar spears,

00:05:28.194 --> 00:05:29.394
I'm gonna load them
into our air cannon

00:05:29.494 --> 00:05:32.465
here, and fire them
into this wooden target.

00:05:32.565 --> 00:05:33.399
 Oh my.

00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:35.034
 We'll see how
deep it penetrates,

00:05:35.101 --> 00:05:38.070
how well it holds up, and if
it holds up while I remove

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:39.171
the spear from the target.

00:05:39.271 --> 00:05:40.105
 Awesome.

00:05:40.172 --> 00:05:41.240
 Clayton, you're up first.

00:05:41.307 --> 00:05:42.007
You ready?

00:05:42.107 --> 00:05:42.709
 I am.

00:05:51.583 --> 00:05:58.858
DAVID BAKER: In three, two, one.

00:06:09.134 --> 00:06:09.769
Whew.

00:06:20.713 --> 00:06:23.082
Well, Clayton, your
spear really dug in deep.

00:06:23.148 --> 00:06:24.916
It went in about four inches.

00:06:25.017 --> 00:06:26.085
Came out fine.

00:06:26.152 --> 00:06:28.854
Everything still
tight and dialed in.

00:06:28.921 --> 00:06:31.357
This is interesting,
being offset.

00:06:31.457 --> 00:06:32.724 line:0%
 Being that it's
a boar spear, I

00:06:32.824 --> 00:06:34.426 line:0%
wanted to make sure
that whatever laceration

00:06:34.527 --> 00:06:36.429 line:0%
the blade produced
on the animal,

00:06:36.496 --> 00:06:38.731 line:0%
wasn't wide enough to allow
the wings to come through.

00:06:38.831 --> 00:06:40.065 line:0%
And the best way
to do that was just

00:06:40.132 --> 00:06:41.900 line:7%
to make them perpendicular.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:42.668
 All right.

00:06:42.735 --> 00:06:43.301
Nicely done.

00:06:43.368 --> 00:06:44.303
 Thank you.

00:06:44.403 --> 00:06:45.772
DAVID BAKER: Allen, you're up.

00:06:50.776 --> 00:06:52.177
A little warp in your shaft.

00:06:52.277 --> 00:06:54.346
I'm picking it up
in the tube there,

00:06:54.413 --> 00:06:58.016
so hopefully it
will all fly well.

00:06:58.116 --> 00:07:02.889
All right in three, two, one.

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:27.079
All right, Allen, that
buried itself quite nicely.

00:07:27.179 --> 00:07:30.515
Went in to about there,
again, about four inches deep.

00:07:30.582 --> 00:07:31.917 line:7%
Like the leaf shape on this.

00:07:32.017 --> 00:07:34.454 line:0%
I like the fact that you
hand-hammered to the socket.

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:36.522
I was kind of worried when
I put this in our machine,

00:07:36.589 --> 00:07:39.826
that slight warp in your
shaft there would bind up.

00:07:39.892 --> 00:07:42.128
But not a problem.

00:07:42.228 --> 00:07:43.228
Good job.

00:07:43.295 --> 00:07:44.563
 Thank you.

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:46.499
WIL WILLIS: Bladesmiths.

00:07:46.566 --> 00:07:50.402
Both of you have turned
in fantastic boar spears.

00:07:50.502 --> 00:07:52.438
However in this
arena of competition,

00:07:52.538 --> 00:07:54.607
there can only be one
Forged in Fire champion.

00:08:00.112 --> 00:08:01.414
Allen.

00:08:01.514 --> 00:08:03.649
Congratulations, you are
the Forged in Fire champion.

00:08:03.716 --> 00:08:04.583
CLAYTON: Congratulations.

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:06.319
ALLEN: You did awesome.

00:08:06.419 --> 00:08:08.187
WIL WILLIS: Clayton.

00:08:08.253 --> 00:08:09.489
Please surrender your weapon.

00:08:11.824 --> 00:08:13.825
CLAYTON: Right now, I guess I
am a little bit disappointed.

00:08:13.892 --> 00:08:15.761
But this is the
first spear I made.

00:08:15.828 --> 00:08:18.064
And for me, it's
really important

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:19.698
that it was such a tight race.

00:08:19.765 --> 00:08:21.433
I don't want it to be
anything that I handed him,

00:08:21.533 --> 00:08:23.235
or anything that he
would have handed me.

00:08:23.335 --> 00:08:25.137
Allen is a deserving champion.

00:08:25.238 --> 00:08:26.572
WIL WILLIS: Allen,
congratulations.

00:08:26.639 --> 00:08:28.274
You are the Forged
in Fire champion

00:08:28.374 --> 00:08:31.076
and with that comes
a check for $10,000.

00:08:31.143 --> 00:08:32.043
Congratulations.

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:32.778
 Thank you.

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:36.982
DAVID BAKER: You know what I
really appreciate about that,

00:08:37.082 --> 00:08:40.319
is the fact that you
took the time to actually

00:08:40.386 --> 00:08:41.787
hammer that cone out.

00:08:41.853 --> 00:08:44.623
Making that socket is a
really nice touch on any kind

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:46.091
of traditional spear.

00:08:46.158 --> 00:08:47.193
It's well done.

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:49.194
 I am the Forged
in Fire champion.

00:08:49.261 --> 00:08:50.763
I cannot believe it.

00:08:50.863 --> 00:08:54.866
I had no idea if it was going
to be me or him, and it was me.

00:08:54.966 --> 00:08:56.702
I'm betting being a
Forged in Fire champion

00:08:56.802 --> 00:08:58.137
is going to help
this stay-at-home dad

00:08:58.237 --> 00:09:00.673
slash knife maker, convince
a few people that they

00:09:00.773 --> 00:09:01.807
should try out my blades.

00:09:01.908 --> 00:09:04.143
 That's great.

